			<div class="content" id="post-content-2390947">
									

<p>My understanding of what's going on here:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I don't think they're 'literally' computers, so much as the fact that their current structure easily lends itself as a comparison to one.</p>
<p>Essentially, they're not so much an entity from the previous universe so much as the leftovers of an entity from the previous universe. Basically the prior entities embody concepts and bits that simply can't exist in the current one. Most of the prior entities reduced themselves to some very core concepts that could exist, but in doing so lost all their prior knowledge and identities, but at the core retained the essence of themselves. (It's suggested that they're now 'us' in the sense of stuff that's alive on the planet)</p>
<p>But the ones here were too prideful or fearful to sacrifice that much of themselves and tried to fit themselves in via a loophole. Exceepppt, they didn't to a great job and in fitting through said loophole, great chunks of 'self' were annihilated to fit. It's also why they're so weird and basically a kludged together bunch of identities that exist in the interaction between Pandas and Bamboo, and why they're so maimed. But it's also why they remember the previous universe.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Note, I do think the prose is too purple and somewhat impenetrable - but that's my understanding of what's going on.</p>

							</div>

								
					
