<blockquote>
<p>I can't understand the logic in thinking they are computers. Their structure may lend themselves to be similar to a network, but does it really mean they are?</p>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cybernetics">Cybernetics</a>, dude- it'll blow your mind. X3</p>
<p>Basically, anything that can receive input, process that input, and respond in some way is a control system: any network connecting enough control systems to achieve some level of recursive feedback is a cybernetic system. Network enough of those together, and you have a computer. There is technically no meaningful difference between &quot;computer&quot; and &quot;network&quot;; its simply a matter of words: whether you mean the box on your desk, or the network of connections that it holds and links to the global network of connections operated by organic networks of connections.</p>

